    <paragraphs>
      <title>Sources</title>
      <p>
        This book is a remix of the sources listed below. I have edited these sources to a greater or lesser degree as well as added original material. Each source's license is listed with it; material within a source may carry its own exceptions, as noted in that source's attributions.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <url href="https://www.pcc.edu/math-in-society/"
                visual="pcc.edu/math-in-society"><q>Math In Society, 2nd Edition: Tools for decision making</q></url>
              by Lee et al., Portland Community College Math Department, licensed under
              <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0" visual="creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</url>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <url href="https://www.opentextbookstore.com/mathinsociety/"><q>Math in Society</q></url> by David Lippman, licensed under
              <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0" visual="creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</url>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <url href="https://www.deanza.edu/faculty/bloomroberta/math11/index.html"><q>Applied Finite Mathematics 3rd ed</q></url> by R. Sekhon and R. Bloom, De Anza College, licensed under
              <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0" visual="creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0">CC BY 4.0</url>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <url href="https://stats.optimabooks.org"><q>OPTIMA Statistics</q></url> by Jonathan Duncan, Walla Walla University. Its original license is CC BY-NC 4.0; material from it is used here under CC BY-SA 4.0 with the author's permission.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        The git log for this book contains granular attribution of sources. In broad strokes, the Finance and Logical Reasoning chapters draw heavily from Lee et al. In particular, the sections on Income Taxes, Logical Fallacies, and Expected Value are brought over from Lee et al. with only brief edits or corrections. The exercises in the Income Taxes and Logical Fallacies sections also come from Lee et al., and those in the Propositions and Logical Operators section are adapted from Lee et al. Exercises in other sections are my own, drawn from my course materials. Lee et al. was itself in part derived from Lippman's work; in places I have used the original source material rather than their adaptation as a basis for my work here. The sections on sets contain material from Sekhon and Bloom as well as Duncan. The sections on Probability and Statistics draw heavily on Duncan's work.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Use of AI</title>
      <p>
        I wrote and assembled this book myself. While writing it in 2025, I used Google's Gemini to proofread some passages and to help draw some of the figures. Beginning in fall 2026, I used Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, more extensively to help revise it: checking calculations, finding typos and inconsistencies, suggesting clearer wording, drafting index entries and image descriptions, and drafting exercises adapted from my own course materials. I reviewed every change and decided what to keep; responsibility for the content, including any remaining errors, is mine.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Thanks</title>
      <p>
        I thank the authors listed above for their work and for making it public. Without them, this project would not have been completed in the time I had to work on it. I have freely edited, extended, and adapted these sources. Any mistakes are no doubt mine!
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, I want to thank <url href="https://www.wallawalla.edu/" visual="wallawalla.edu">Walla Walla University</url> for awarding me a sabbatical during Spring Quarter of 2025 to work on this project. This book would not have been possible without relief from both my teaching and administrative duties.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </preface>

